const STAGES=[["IT Foundations","Computers, operating systems, hardware"],["Networking","TCP/IP, DNS, HTTP, routing, firewalls"],["Linux & Windows","Command line, permissions, administration"],["Programming","Python, scripting, SQL, Git"],["Security Fundamentals","CIA triad, threats, vulnerabilities, cryptography"],["Defensive Security","SOC, SIEM, monitoring, incident response"],["Offensive Security","Vulnerability assessment, authorised testing"],["Specialisations","Cloud, forensics, AppSec, GRC, architecture"]];

const TUTS=[
{id:"t-net",st:2,title:"Networking fundamentals",obj:["Explain how data moves across a network","Describe IP, ports, TCP/UDP, DNS and HTTP","Spot where security controls sit"],
body:`<p>A network is devices exchanging data using agreed rules (protocols). Data is split into <b>packets</b>, each carrying source and destination addresses.</p>
<h3>Key building blocks</h3>
<table><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>IP address</td><td>Logical address of a device (IPv4 e.g. 192.168.1.10)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Port</td><td>Identifies a service on a host: 22 SSH, 53 DNS, 80 HTTP, 443 HTTPS</td></tr>
<tr><td>TCP</td><td>Reliable, connection-based (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)</td></tr>
<tr><td>UDP</td><td>Fast, connectionless (DNS queries, streaming)</td></tr>
<tr><td>DNS</td><td>Translates names like example.com into IP addresses</td></tr>
<tr><td>DHCP</td><td>Automatically hands out IP configuration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Firewall</td><td>Allows or blocks traffic using rules on IPs, ports and protocols</td></tr></table>
<h3>Security view</h3><p>Attackers look for exposed ports, unencrypted protocols (HTTP, Telnet) and DNS abuse. Defenders monitor traffic, segment networks and close what is not needed.</p>
<div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> assuming a closed port means a secure host. Services behind allowed ports can still be vulnerable.</div>`,
q:["Which protocol encrypts web traffic?",["HTTP","HTTPS","Telnet"],1]},
{id:"t-sub",st:2,title:"IP addressing and subnetting",obj:["Read CIDR notation","Calculate network, broadcast and usable hosts","Use the Subnet Calculator lab"],
body:`<p>CIDR notation like <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> means the first 24 bits identify the network; the remaining 8 bits identify hosts.</p>
<p><b>Usable hosts = 2<sup>(32 − prefix)</sup> − 2</b> (network and broadcast addresses are reserved). A /24 gives 254 hosts; a /26 gives 62.</p>
<h3>Private ranges (RFC 1918)</h3><p><code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>, <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>: not routable on the public internet.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Why it matters:</b> subnets let you segment networks, so a compromised laptop cannot reach the database server directly.</div>
<div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> forgetting to subtract the network and broadcast addresses.</div>`,
q:["How many usable hosts are in a /26?",["30","62","64"],1]},
{id:"t-lin",st:3,title:"Linux command line and permissions",obj:["Navigate the filesystem","Read permission strings like -rwxr-x---","Use grep to search logs"],
body:`<p>Linux runs most servers and security tools. Essential commands: <code>pwd</code>, <code>ls -l</code>, <code>cd</code>, <code>cat</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>chmod</code>, <code>whoami</code>.</p>
<h3>Permissions</h3><p><code>-rwxr-x---</code> = file type, then owner (rwx), group (r-x), others (---). Numeric: r=4, w=2, x=1, so <code>chmod 750</code> gives rwxr-x---.</p>
<h3>Least privilege</h3><p>Give users and files only the access they need. World-writable files and scripts running as root are classic privilege-escalation paths.</p>
<div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> using <code>chmod 777</code> to fix a problem. It grants everyone full access.</div>`,
q:["What does chmod 640 give the owner?",["Read and write","Read, write, execute","Read only"],0]},
{id:"t-cia",st:5,title:"CIA triad, threats and vulnerabilities",obj:["Define confidentiality, integrity, availability","Tell threats, vulnerabilities and risk apart","Recognise common attack types"],
body:`<p><b>Confidentiality:</b> only authorised people see data. <b>Integrity:</b> data is accurate and unaltered. <b>Availability:</b> systems work when needed.</p>
<h3>Terms</h3><p>A <b>vulnerability</b> is a weakness. A <b>threat</b> is something that can exploit it. <b>Risk</b> = likelihood × impact.</p>
<h3>Common attacks</h3><p>Phishing (social engineering), malware and ransomware, brute force and credential stuffing, SQL injection, DDoS, insider misuse.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Example:</b> ransomware mainly attacks availability; a data leak attacks confidentiality; tampered financial records attack integrity.</div>`,
q:["Ransomware primarily harms which property?",["Availability","Confidentiality","Non-repudiation"],0]},
{id:"t-crypto",st:5,title:"Cryptography essentials",obj:["Compare symmetric and asymmetric encryption","Explain hashing and its uses","Understand TLS at a high level"],
body:`<p><b>Symmetric</b> (AES): one shared key, fast, used for bulk data. <b>Asymmetric</b> (RSA, ECC): public/private key pair, used for key exchange and signatures.</p>
<p><b>Hashing</b> (SHA-256) produces a fixed fingerprint; it cannot be reversed and is used for integrity checks. Passwords should be stored with slow, salted hashes (bcrypt, Argon2), never plain text or fast hashes.</p>
<p><b>TLS</b> uses asymmetric crypto to agree a session key, then symmetric crypto to protect the traffic (the padlock in your browser).</p>
<div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> calling hashing "encryption". Encryption is reversible with a key; hashing is not.</div>`,
q:["Which is best for storing passwords?",["AES encryption","Salted slow hash (bcrypt)","Base64 encoding"],1]},
{id:"t-soc",st:6,title:"SOC operations, logs and incident response",obj:["Describe what a SOC analyst does","Read authentication logs","Follow the incident response lifecycle"],
body:`<p>SOC analysts watch alerts from a <b>SIEM</b> (e.g. Splunk), triage them as true or false positives, investigate and escalate.</p>
<h3>Signs in logs</h3><p>Many failed logins from one IP then a success (brute force), logins at odd hours, impossible travel, new admin accounts.</p>
<h3>Incident response lifecycle</h3><p>1 Preparation, 2 Detection and analysis, 3 Containment, 4 Eradication, 5 Recovery, 6 Lessons learned.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Practice:</b> try the Terminal and Log Hunt labs, then write a short incident report.</div>`,
q:["What comes right after detection and analysis?",["Containment","Preparation","Lessons learned"],0]},
{id:"t-py",st:4,title:"Python for security automation",obj:["Parse logs with Python","Automate repetitive checks","Know safe scripting practice"],
body:`<p>Python is the glue of security work: parsing logs, calling APIs, enriching alerts, writing detections.</p><p><code>import re, collections</code> then count failed logins per IP: read lines, use <code>re.findall(r'from (\\S+)', line)</code>, feed a <code>collections.Counter</code>, print <code>.most_common(5)</code>.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Use <code>requests</code> for threat-intel APIs (VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB), <code>hashlib</code> for file hashing, <code>argparse</code> for tools, and virtual environments for dependencies. Never hard-code API keys; use environment variables.</p><div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> running untrusted scripts or storing secrets in Git.</div>`,
q:["Which module counts frequency of items easily?",["collections.Counter","os.path","time"],0]},
{id:"t-win",st:3,title:"Windows security and Active Directory",obj:["Understand users, groups and policies","Know key event IDs","Recognise AD attack paths"],
body:`<p>Most enterprises run Windows with <b>Active Directory (AD)</b> managing identities. Group Policy enforces settings across machines.</p><table><tr><th>Event ID</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>4624 / 4625</td><td>Successful / failed logon</td></tr><tr><td>4672</td><td>Admin privileges assigned</td></tr><tr><td>4688</td><td>New process created</td></tr><tr><td>4720</td><td>User account created</td></tr></table><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Common AD abuses: password spraying, Kerberoasting, pass-the-hash and over-privileged service accounts. Defences: tiered admin model, LAPS, MFA, disabling legacy NTLM, monitoring privileged groups.</p>`,
q:["Which event ID is a failed logon?",["4624","4625","4688"],1]},
{id:"t-web",st:7,title:"Web application security (OWASP Top 10)",obj:["Explain SQL injection, XSS and broken access control","Apply secure coding fixes","Spot attacks in logs"],
body:`<p>Web apps are the most attacked surface. The OWASP Top 10 lists the biggest risks.</p><table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>Injection (SQLi)</td><td>Parameterised queries, input validation</td></tr><tr><td>XSS</td><td>Output encoding, Content-Security-Policy</td></tr><tr><td>Broken access control</td><td>Server-side authorisation checks on every request</td></tr><tr><td>Auth failures</td><td>MFA, rate limiting, secure session handling</td></tr><tr><td>Misconfiguration</td><td>Hardening, remove defaults, patching</td></tr></table><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Threat modelling (STRIDE), SAST/DAST in CI pipelines, dependency scanning (SCA), secrets scanning, and secure code review checklists. Test only apps you own or are authorised to test, such as deliberately vulnerable labs.</p>`,
q:["Best defence against SQL injection?",["Parameterised queries","Longer passwords","Hiding the login page"],0]},
{id:"t-pen",st:7,title:"Penetration testing methodology and reporting",obj:["Follow a structured methodology","Understand scope and rules of engagement","Write a useful report"],
body:`<p>Authorised testing follows phases: <b>scoping and written permission, reconnaissance, scanning and enumeration, exploitation, post-exploitation, reporting, retest</b>.</p><h3>Reporting</h3><p>A report has an executive summary, scope, method, findings each with severity (CVSS), evidence, impact and remediation advice. The report is the product; a finding nobody can act on has no value.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Learn Active Directory testing, web API testing, privilege escalation concepts, evasion awareness from the defender side, and purple teaming (testers and defenders working together to improve detections).</p><div class="box w"><b>Rule:</b> no written authorisation, no testing.</div>`,
q:["What must exist before any test begins?",["Written authorisation","A faster laptop","A public IP"],0]},
{id:"t-cloud",st:8,title:"Cloud security",obj:["Apply the shared responsibility model","Secure identity and storage","Know common misconfigurations"],
body:`<p>In the cloud the provider secures the infrastructure; <b>you</b> secure identities, data, configuration and access.</p><p>Top risks: public storage buckets, over-permissive IAM roles, exposed access keys, no MFA on admin accounts, unlogged activity.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Infrastructure as Code scanning (Terraform), CSPM tools, least-privilege IAM policies, key management (KMS), network segmentation with security groups, centralised logging (CloudTrail, Azure Monitor), container and Kubernetes hardening.</p>`,
q:["In the shared responsibility model, who secures your data?",["The provider only","You","Nobody"],1]},
{id:"t-dfir",st:8,title:"Digital forensics and incident response",obj:["Preserve evidence correctly","Apply order of volatility","Build an incident timeline"],
body:`<p>Forensics must be repeatable and defensible. Keep a <b>chain of custody</b>, work on forensic copies, and verify with hashes.</p><p><b>Order of volatility:</b> CPU and RAM, network connections, running processes, disk, logs and backups.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Memory analysis (Volatility), disk timelines, registry and prefetch artefacts on Windows, log correlation, malware triage in sandboxes, and writing a timeline that answers: how they got in, what they touched, how to remove them.</p>`,
q:["Which evidence is most volatile?",["Hard disk","RAM","Backups"],1]},
{id:"t-grc",st:8,title:"Governance, risk and compliance (GRC)",obj:["Know key frameworks","Run a basic risk assessment","Understand data protection law"],
body:`<p>GRC turns security into managed, auditable business practice. Frameworks: <b>ISO 27001</b> (management system), <b>NIST CSF</b> (Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), <b>CIS Controls</b>, <b>PCI DSS</b> for card data.</p><p>Privacy law: <b>POPIA</b> in South Africa and <b>GDPR</b> in the EU require lawful processing, minimisation and breach notification.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Control mapping across frameworks, third-party risk, business impact analysis, audit evidence collection, security awareness programmes and risk registers with owners and deadlines.</p>`,
q:["Which law governs personal data in South Africa?",["GDPR","POPIA","HIPAA"],1]},
{id:"t-hunt",st:6,title:"Threat hunting, MITRE ATT&CK and detection engineering",obj:["Use ATT&CK to describe attacker behaviour","Form a hunting hypothesis","Write a detection"],
body:`<p><b>MITRE ATT&CK</b> catalogues attacker tactics (the why) and techniques (the how): Initial Access, Execution, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Credential Access, Lateral Movement, Exfiltration and more.</p><p>A hunt starts with a hypothesis, e.g. "an attacker is using PowerShell to download tools", then you query logs for it, validate, and convert good hunts into permanent detection rules (Sigma, Splunk SPL).</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Baselining normal behaviour, detection-as-code, tuning false positives, the Pyramid of Pain (hunt behaviours, not just IPs and hashes), and SOAR playbooks for automated response.</p>`,
q:["ATT&CK tactics describe...",["Attacker goals","Firewall rules","Encryption keys"],0]},
{id:"t-arch",st:8,title:"Security architecture and zero trust",obj:["Apply defence in depth","Explain zero trust","Do basic threat modelling"],
body:`<p><b>Defence in depth:</b> layers (perimeter, network, host, application, data, identity) so one failure is not fatal. <b>Zero trust:</b> never trust, always verify; authenticate and authorise every request, assume breach, segment aggressively.</p><h3>Advanced</h3><p>Identity-centric design (SSO, MFA, conditional access), micro-segmentation, secrets management, secure SDLC, backup and recovery design (immutable backups), and threat modelling with STRIDE and data-flow diagrams.</p>`,
q:["Zero trust means...",["Trust the internal network","Verify every request","No passwords"],1]},
{id:"t-win0",st:3,title:"Windows fundamentals",obj:["Navigate Windows folders, services and the registry","Use basic PowerShell","Know where attackers hide"],
body:`<p>Key places: <code>C:\\Users</code> (profiles), <code>C:\\Windows\\System32</code> (core tools), the <b>registry</b> (settings database), <b>services</b> (background programs) and the <b>Event Viewer</b> (logs).</p><h3>PowerShell basics</h3><p><code>Get-Process</code>, <code>Get-Service</code>, <code>Get-LocalUser</code>, <code>Get-WinEvent -LogName Security -MaxEvents 20</code>, <code>Get-ChildItem</code>.</p><h3>Security view</h3><p>Persistence hides in Run keys, scheduled tasks and new services. NTFS permissions and UAC limit damage; Defender and the firewall are your first layer.</p><div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> using an admin account for daily work.</div>`,
q:["Which registry area is often abused to run malware at login?",["Run keys","Fonts","Wallpaper"],0]},
{id:"t-lx",st:3,title:"Linux administration and hardening",obj:["Know the filesystem layout","Inspect processes, ports and services","Harden a server"],
body:`<p>Layout: <code>/etc</code> config, <code>/var/log</code> logs, <code>/home</code> users, <code>/tmp</code> temporary files, <code>/usr/bin</code> programs.</p><p>Inspect: <code>ps aux</code>, <code>ss -tulpn</code> (open ports), <code>systemctl status</code>, <code>journalctl -u ssh</code>, <code>crontab -l</code>, <code>find / -perm -4000</code> (SUID files).</p><h3>Hardening checklist</h3><p>SSH keys instead of passwords, disable root login, firewall (ufw), automatic updates, remove unused services, least-privilege sudo, centralised logs.</p><div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> leaving a root cron job that runs a writable script.</div>`,
q:["Which directory holds most system logs?",["/var/log","/etc/log","/home/log"],0]},
{id:"t-eh",st:7,title:"Ethical hacking: from recon to proof",obj:["Follow the phases of an authorised test","Tell passive from active recon","Prove impact with minimal harm"],
body:`<p>Phases: <b>scope and permission, recon, scanning and enumeration, vulnerability analysis, controlled exploitation, post-exploitation analysis, cleanup, reporting</b>.</p><p><b>Passive recon</b> uses public information only (WHOIS, DNS records, search engines). <b>Active recon</b> touches the target (for example a service scan with Nmap) and needs permission.</p><p>Practise only in your own lab or platforms built for it (TryHackMe, deliberately vulnerable VMs, DVWA). Prove a flaw with the smallest safe demonstration, never destroy or keep data, then document everything.</p><div class="box w"><b>Rule:</b> stay inside scope and stop when told to.</div>`,
q:["Passive recon means...",["Touching target systems","Using public information only","Exploiting bugs"],1]},
{id:"t-report",st:7,title:"Report writing for security work",obj:["Structure a finding or incident report","Write for managers and engineers","Give fixable recommendations"],
body:`<p>A report is what the client pays for. Write for two readers: managers (risk, business impact) and engineers (how to reproduce and fix).</p><h3>Structure</h3><p><b>Executive summary</b>, scope and method, <b>findings</b> (title, severity, description, evidence, impact, recommendation), timeline (incidents), conclusion. Rate severity consistently (CVSS or Critical, High, Medium, Low).</p><h3>Good habits</h3><p>Facts over opinion, screenshots and log lines as evidence, one clear fix per finding, no jargon in the summary. Try the final CTF challenge.</p><div class="box w"><b>Common mistake:</b> pasting raw tool output with no explanation or fix.</div>`,
q:["Who mainly reads the executive summary?",["Management","Only the tester","Attackers"],0]
},
{id:'t-ir',st:6,title:'Incident response playbooks (NIST SP 800-61)',obj:['Run an incident with defined roles and severity levels','Follow a ransomware playbook','Measure response with MTTD and MTTR'],
body:`<p>Mature teams do not improvise. A playbook lists triggers, owners, steps and escalation. Roles: <b>incident commander</b>, technical lead, communications lead and a scribe who keeps the timeline.</p><table><tr><th>Severity</th><th>Example</th><th>Response</th></tr><tr><td>P1 Critical</td><td>Ransomware, data breach</td><td>Immediate, 24/7, executive briefing</td></tr><tr><td>P2 High</td><td>Admin account compromised</td><td>Within 1 hour</td></tr><tr><td>P3 Medium</td><td>Single malware infection</td><td>Same day</td></tr><tr><td>P4 Low</td><td>Policy violation</td><td>Ticket queue</td></tr></table><h3>Ransomware playbook</h3><p>Isolate affected hosts (do not power off if memory evidence is needed), identify patient zero, block IOCs, reset exposed credentials, restore from immutable backups, then notify regulators and affected people as law requires (POPIA, GDPR).</p><h3>Metrics</h3><p><b>MTTD</b> mean time to detect, <b>MTTR</b> mean time to respond or recover, <b>RTO</b> and <b>RPO</b> for recovery targets. Finish every incident with a blameless post-incident review and tracked actions.</p>`,
q:['Which metric measures how fast you find an incident?',['MTTR','MTTD','RPO'],1]},
{id:'t-vuln',st:5,title:'Vulnerability management in practice',obj:['Run the vulnerability management cycle','Prioritise beyond CVSS','Set remediation SLAs'],
body:`<p>Cycle: <b>inventory assets, scan (Nessus, OpenVAS, Qualys), prioritise, remediate, verify, report</b>. You cannot protect what you have not inventoried.</p><p><b>CVSS</b> scores severity from 0 to 10 (Critical 9.0 to 10.0, High 7.0 to 8.9, Medium 4.0 to 6.9, Low below 4). Real priority also needs: is it exploited in the wild (CISA KEV list), exploit probability (EPSS), is the asset internet-facing, and how critical is the asset.</p><h3>Typical SLAs</h3><p>Critical and exploited: 72 hours to 7 days. High: 30 days. Medium: 90 days. Document exceptions with compensating controls and an expiry date.</p><div class='box w'><b>Common mistake:</b> patching by score alone and ignoring exposed, actively exploited flaws.</div>`,
q:['What should drive patch priority besides CVSS?',['Alphabetical order','Exploitation in the wild and asset exposure','Vendor logo'],1]},
{id:'t-ndef',st:6,title:'Network defence: segmentation, IDS/IPS and monitoring',obj:['Design segmented networks','Compare IDS and IPS','Know what network telemetry to collect'],
body:`<p><b>Segmentation</b> (VLANs, firewalls, a DMZ for public servers) limits lateral movement. <b>Egress filtering</b> stops malware calling home. <b>ZTNA or VPN</b> secures remote access.</p><p><b>IDS</b> (Suricata, Snort) detects and alerts; <b>IPS</b> sits inline and can block. A rule has an action, protocol, source, destination and options such as a message and signature ID. Zeek and NetFlow give metadata for hunting without storing every packet.</p><h3>What to log</h3><p>Firewall allows and denies, DNS queries, proxy logs, VPN logins, DHCP leases. Forward to a SIEM with accurate time sync (NTP) so timelines line up.</p>`,
q:['What is the key difference between IDS and IPS?',['IPS can block traffic inline','IDS blocks inline','They are identical'],0]},
{id:'t-iam',st:8,title:'Identity and access management (IAM, MFA, PAM)',obj:['Separate authentication from authorisation','Choose strong MFA','Apply least privilege over the account lifecycle'],
body:`<p><b>Authentication</b> proves who you are; <b>authorisation</b> decides what you may do. Use SSO (SAML, OIDC) so identity is managed centrally.</p><p>MFA strength: SMS and email codes are weakest, authenticator apps are better, <b>FIDO2 security keys and passkeys</b> resist phishing. NIST 800-63B favours long passphrases and breach checks over forced complexity.</p><h3>Governance</h3><p>RBAC or ABAC for access, <b>joiner-mover-leaver</b> processes so access follows role changes, periodic access reviews, <b>PAM</b> vaults with session recording for admins, and no shared or unmanaged service accounts.</p>`,
q:['Which MFA method is most phishing-resistant?',['SMS codes','Email codes','FIDO2 security keys'],2]},
{id:'t-mal',st:8,title:'Malware analysis fundamentals',obj:['Separate static from dynamic analysis','Extract indicators of compromise','Work safely in an isolated lab'],
body:`<p><b>Static analysis:</b> hash the file (SHA-256), check reputation, read strings, inspect headers and imports, never execute. <b>Dynamic analysis:</b> run in an isolated sandbox VM (snapshot, no real network) and watch processes, registry changes, files and network beacons.</p><p>Outputs: <b>IOCs</b> (hashes, domains, IPs, mutexes) for blocking and hunting, and <b>YARA</b> rules that describe malware families. Map behaviour to ATT&CK techniques in your report.</p><div class='box w'><b>Safety rule:</b> never analyse live malware on your daily machine or a network-connected host.</div>`,
q:['What is a safe first step with a suspicious file?',['Run it on your laptop','Hash it and check its reputation','Email it to colleagues'],1]},
{id:'t-devsec',st:7,title:'DevSecOps and secure software supply chain',obj:['Shift security left in CI/CD','Name the pipeline security checks','Understand SBOMs and supply-chain risk'],
body:`<p>Build security into the pipeline: pre-commit <b>secrets scanning</b>, <b>SAST</b> on code, <b>SCA</b> on dependencies, container and IaC scanning, <b>DAST</b> against staging, signed build artifacts, protected branches and mandatory code review.</p><p>An <b>SBOM</b> (software bill of materials) lists every component, so when a library such as Log4j is hit by a new flaw you know within minutes which systems are affected. Frameworks: OWASP SAMM, NIST SSDF, SLSA.</p><div class='box w'><b>Common mistake:</b> adding scanners but never triaging or enforcing results.</div>`,
q:['What does an SBOM list?',['Software components','Staff salaries','Open network ports'],0]
}
];

